    "created_at": "2016-05-08T07:33:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-05-08T07:33:17Z",
    "body": "> it's the same as in XML\n\nWell the element _name_ is the same as what you’d get when parsing it as XML, but you still can’t get that markup as-is into a well-formed XML document, right? And in HTML the element that ends up in the DOM is still something you can’t represent in XML, right? I mean this:\n\n``` xml\n<foo) a=\"0;\" <=\"\" script=\"\"></foo>\n```\n\nSo I’m not sure what sort of sameness you mean.\n\nRegardless, that example is not going to result in useful script content now anyway, so I would expect that if we changed the HTML spec to say that SVG `script` in an HTML document should be parsed as a raw text element, then the parsing of the example would change, and it would not break anything—other than the script would now just work as the author appears to have expected, rather than not working\n\nAnyway, this leads me to ask what SVG-in-HTML documents we could actually break if we changed the HTML spec to say that SVG `script` in an HTML document should be parsed as a raw text element. As far as I can see, it would just cause the content of some SVG `script` elements in existing HTML documents to actually execute as expected, whereas they don’t now. Do we want to avoid that?\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
